      "timestamp": "2026-09-30T22:27:12.189Z",
      "message": "This is a one-line edit, but it lands on rung 1 of the ladder: session lifetime is security posture, so I'm flagging the consequence before touching it rather than after.\n\n**The change:** `config.js:3`, `SESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES: 30` → `480`.\n\n**The consequence:** a 16× longer window in which a stolen or abandoned session stays valid. Concretely — a session left open on a shared or unattended machine stays usable for the rest of a workday, and a hijacked session token (XSS, stolen cookie, shoulder-surfed device) keeps working for 8 hours instead of 30 minutes. `server.js:2` suggests this value is the only thing gating expiry, so there's no idle-timeout or re-auth backstop in this repo to soften it.\n\nIf the goal is \"users shouldn't get logged out mid-work,\" the usual answer is a sliding/idle timeout (30 min idle, refreshed on activity, with an absolute cap) rather than an 8-hour absolute one — that keeps the convenience without the exposure window. That's a design choice, so it's a separate conversation if you want it.\n\nConfirm you want the flat 30 → 480 and I'll make exactly that edit, nothing else.",
